<h2 id="logs">Logs, monitoring and audit records</h2>
<p><b>Privacy approach:</b> logging follows the data minimization principle (KVKK, GDPR). Who accessed personal data, when and from where can be traced in
    a separate audit stream; records hold no more personal data than the task needs, and everything stays on your server and is never sent to us. You are the
    data controller: you decide retention periods, access rights and privacy notices according to your organization's policy.</p>
<p>Services send their logs, traces and metrics to <b>OpenObserve</b> with OpenTelemetry. Open the UI in a browser on the local network:
    <code>http://&lt;server&gt;:5080</code> (<code>OPENOBSERVE_PORT</code>). Sign in with <code>OPENOBSERVE_ADMIN_EMAIL</code> and
    <code>OPENOBSERVE_ADMIN_PASSWORD</code> from <code>.env</code>; the setup script generates the password and shows it at the end. Services write with the same e-mail / password; change the password only in <code>.env</code> and run <code>docker compose up -d</code>. Do not expose this port to the internet.</p>
<table>
    <thead><tr><th>Stream</th><th>Content</th><th>Retention</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>booster</code></td><td>Operational logs (warnings, errors, info): filter by service, level, <code>trace_id</code>.</td>
            <td><code>LOG_RETENTION_DAYS</code> (default 30 days)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>audit</code></td><td>Audit records: who did what, when and from where (below).</td>
            <td>We recommend 2 years: in the UI <b>Streams › audit › Settings › Data retention</b> = 730</td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>
<p>The <b>Traces</b> menu shows a request's path gateway → IdP → Management → AuthZ and the duration of each step; the <code>trace_id</code> on a log
    line takes you to its trace.</p>
<h3>Audit records</h3>
<ul>
    <li><b>Authentication:</b> successful sign-in (with method: password, LDAP, e-mail / SMS code, external provider), failed sign-in and its reason, account
        lockout, wrong verification / two-step code, sign-out and sign-out everywhere, refresh token reuse (a sign of a stolen token).</li>
    <li><b>Account:</b> password reset and change, e-mail / phone change, signing out other sessions, invitation accepted, account deletion, suspend / activate,
        consent granted / denied.</li>
    <li><b>Administration:</b> every change made in the console or with a service account (users, roles, scopes, applications, pools, tenants, permissions,
        legal texts, license) and reads of screens with personal data (user list, sessions, consents).</li>
</ul>
<p>A record contains the person's id (<code>sub</code>), pool / application id, IP address, browser and outcome. <b>E-mail, phone, name, password, codes and
    tokens are never written</b>; for an attempt with an unknown user name the value entered is kept only as a keyed digest. E-mails, phones, tokens and codes in
    operational logs are masked before they are sent. Audit records contain personal data such as IP addresses, so restrict access to the OpenObserve
    administrator and set the retention according to your organization's personal data retention and deletion policy.</p>
<p><b>Notification records:</b> the recipient and content of sent e-mails / SMS are stored encrypted in the database and deleted after 30 days.</p>
<p><b>Turning it off or sending to your own system:</b> to turn OpenObserve off, empty <code>COMPOSE_PROFILES</code> and <code>OTEL_ENDPOINT</code> in
    <code>.env</code>; logs then stay only in <code>docker compose logs</code>. To send to your own OTLP/HTTP receiver (Grafana, Elastic, Datadog …), set
    <code>OTEL_ENDPOINT</code> and <code>OTEL_HEADERS</code> to its address and credentials.</p>

<h2 id="updates">Updates</h2>
<div class="code"><pre>docker compose pull
docker compose up -d --remove-orphans</pre></div>
<p>The database schema is updated automatically on start. <code>--remove-orphans</code> also removes the containers of services dropped in the new
    release. To pin a specific version, change <code>BOOSTER_VERSION</code> in <code>.env</code>.</p>
<p><b>Older installations:</b> Booster Auth no longer uses Kafka. If you updated from a release that included Kafka, you can also delete the old Kafka
    data: <code>docker volume rm booster-auth_kafka_data</code> (the prefix is <code>COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME</code> from <code>.env</code>).</p>

<h2 id="backup">Backups</h2>
<p>All persistent data lives in the installation folder. For a consistent backup:</p>
<ol>
    <li><code>docker compose down</code></li>
    <li>Back up the <code>data/</code>, <code>pki/</code> and <code>secrets/</code> folders and the <code>.env</code> file.</li>
    <li><code>docker compose up -d</code></li>
</ol>
<div class="callout warn"><p><b>A backup without <code>.env</code> is useless.</b> Passwords and encrypted data are protected with the keys in this file.</p></div>

<h2 id="email">E-mail settings</h2>
<p>Fill in <code>MAIL_SENDER</code>, <code>MAIL_SMTP_HOST</code>, <code>MAIL_PORT</code>, <code>MAIL_USERNAME</code>, <code>MAIL_PASSWORD</code> and
    <code>MAIL_ENABLE_SSL</code> in <code>.env</code>, then run <code>docker compose up -d</code>.</p>

<h2 id="problems">Common problems</h2>
<h3>The browser shows a certificate warning</h3>
<p>Expected if the locally generated CA has not been trusted. Add <code>pki/trust/ca.crt</code> as a trusted root certificate, or use your own
    certificate in production (<a href="/en/docs/installation#https">Installation › HTTPS</a>).</p>
<h3>A "redirect_uri" error after sign-in</h3>
<p>The <code>redirect_uri</code> your application sends must match a registered URI exactly (scheme, port and trailing <code>/</code> included). If the
    pool's redirect domain policy is on, the domain must also be allowed.</p>
<h3>The console is read-only</h3>
<p>The license is missing, expired or invalid. Check the state on <b>System &gt; License</b> (<a href="/en/docs/licensing#states">License states</a>).</p>
<h3>Sign-up e-mails do not arrive</h3>
<p>Without SMTP settings no e-mail is sent. Use <code>docker compose logs booster-notification</code> to see delivery errors.</p>
<p>Notifications are sent without waiting for a reply and a failed send is <b>not retried</b>: if the Notification service is down or the SMTP server
    cannot be reached at that moment, the e-mail is lost. The user has to request the code or link again. When the service could not be reached,
    <code>docker compose logs booster-idp</code> shows a warning containing <code>yeniden denenmeyecek</code>.</p>
<h3>A service does not start</h3>
<p>Check the status with <code>docker compose ps</code> and the error with <code>docker compose logs &lt;service&gt;</code>. If a value in <code>.env</code>
    still contains <code>CHANGE_ME</code>, the preparation step names the missing variable: <code>docker compose logs booster-init</code>.</p>
